data=[
('KATO-ORIGINAL-LATTICE',['kato'],13366,13393,[3],'(8.1.3),sections8.2,8.9,8.11 and13.6–13.7,pp180–185,227','The retained-smoothed classes lie in the original integral image lattice, and the modular-symbol span has nonzero projections to both Betti sign lines.','Fixed weight-two form and original integral coefficient lattice; allowed retained smoothing and symbol indices.','The rational map to the fixed T2E, choice of one integral combination outside both kernels and clearing its fixed denominators are local deductions, not a uniformity theorem for varying newforms.'),
('KATO-SMOOTHED-RECIPROCITY',['kato'],13394,13415,[4,8],'(4.2.4),section5.2,Theorems6.6 and9.7,pp143,163,189','The dual exponential of the retained-smoothed class agrees with the retained-smoothed de Rham zeta value and its primitive character-weighted period formula.','k=2,r=rprime=1; c,d_s>1 prime to6NF with the required congruences; actual primitive character and original form.','No smoother or character-group order is divided out. The valuation2e, signed h factor and discriminant4h basis comparison are local calculations using this formula.'),
('KATO-NORM-RELATION',['kato'],13452,13496,[6,7,11],'Proposition8.12,section13.1 and(5.1.1),(5.2.1–2)','Corestriction of the original classes has exactly the Euler factors at newly introduced good support primes, with no factor for repeated prime powers.','The original retained class, symbol and smoothing choice at each permitted index; compatible integral transfer and coefficient action.','The twisting equivariance inserts chi(q) and q inverse in the displayed K9. Extension to the1999 index set and identity of its class are separately proved; no proportionality-based replacement class.'),
('KATO-RATIONAL-DIVISIBILITY',['kato'],13678,13728,[10,11],'Theorems13.4(1),(2),13.5(2),13.6–13.7 and(12.2.1–2),pp220,224–227','The rational Euler-system bound controls the subscript-zero H2 by the coordinate ideal of the supplied nonzero Euler system, under the stated rational representation hypotheses.','Odd rank-two representation, appropriate purity, rational irreducibility, nonidentity unipotent with one-dimensional fixed space, nonzero generic sign components; localize away from2.','The p=2 use additionally consumes the separately accepted published1999 bridge. Part13.4(3) and its residual/integral hypotheses are not used. Full H2 requires the separate local-cone correction.'),
('KATO-LOCAL-IW-FINITENESS',['kato'],13863,13935,[13],'(12.2.3),p220','The stated local Iwasawa cohomology module has the finiteness and torsion properties used for the fixed old-support correction.','The actual fixed elliptic representation, local support and local quadratic type; rational local Iwasawa coefficients.','The nonzero central value of F_E is not inferred from generic torsion. It additionally uses local duality, finite local elliptic torsion, derived control and Nakayama as proved in the application.'),
('WALDSPURGER-NONZERO-PACKET',['waldspurgerICM','waldspurger'],14402,14419,[23],'1983Theorem1 andProposition1;1981Proposition18 and proof,pp466–467','A nonzero reference central value supplies a nonzero theta packet member and nonzero reference Fourier functional with the indicated local correspondence.','The fixed modular elliptic representation, prescribed reference quadratic parameter with nonzero central value, and the positive real frequency used here.','The positive frequency selects the holomorphic member. Scalar local types and their projections are constructed separately; no blanket nonvanishing after arbitrary projection.'),
('WALDSPURGER-FULL-EIGENSPACE',['waldspurger'],14530,14622,[25,27,62],'Proposition2,p377;Corollary2 and proof,pp483–484;sectionI.3,pp377–378','On the full good-Hecke eigenspace, the stated same-local-squareclass positive squarefree Fourier coefficients satisfy the literal square identity with the central L-value ratio and character factor.','Nonzero scalar eigenspace supplies H1; positive squarefree pair in the same squareclass at every level prime, including2; reference value nonzero.','No complex conjugation or absolute-value substitution. The same dyadic squareclass removes the use of H2 here. Old multiplicities are retained. Proportional linear functionals and rational phase descent are local deductions.'),
('RAMSEY-SECTION-MODEL',['ramsey'],14623,14667,[26],'Definition3.1,Theorem4.2 andRemark4.4','The half-integral modular-form space has the indicated divisor-sheaf section model on X1(4N_aux).','Characteristic-zero coefficient field containing required characters, cusp data and support-projector values; the stated half-integral weight.','Only flat field extension is used and proved by a finite affine-cover kernel calculation. No arbitrary-ring base change or H1 vanishing in weight3/2 is imported.'),
('RAMSEY-GOOD-HECKE',['ramsey'],14730,14787,[28],'Theorem6.2,p615','The good half-integral-weight Hecke coefficient formula has middle factor psi(p)(-n/p) and last factor p*psi(p squared) at weight3/2.','Ramsey odd weight parameter k=3; good p outside the level and coefficient support; character chi=psi.','The manuscript eigenvalue eta(p)*a_p(E) and psi squared=eta squared give E.12. Includes p dividing n and zero nonintegral n/p squared; all-shell phase and integrality follow by local induction.'),
('WEIL-LOCAL-OPERATORS',['weil'],15217,15410,[36,37,38,39],'I.13–I.14,Theorem2;II.25,II.28,Proposition4','The Weil representation has the stated multiplication, scaled-pullback and Fourier operators; its quadratic-form index obeys orthogonal-sum and quaternion-norm identities.','Nondegenerate quadratic form over Q_p or R, fixed common additive character and its self-dual measure.','The common-cover relations, dyadic normalizations and relative determinant Hilbert symbol are explicitly derived in the application. No group section over all dyadic diagonal units is assumed.'),
('WEIL-GLOBAL-THETA',['weil'],15532,15632,[40,41],'II.27;II.30,Proposition5;III.41,Theorem6','The rational theta functional is invariant with the global rational lifts and the product-formula normalization of the local Weil representations.','A rational quadratic space and adelic Schwartz–Bruhat function with the specified compatible local normalizations.','Positive-definite holomorphy at every cusp and the literal extracted counting coefficients are local arguments. Automorphic invariance alone is not claimed to prove them; Haar factors are not inserted into discrete coefficients.'),
('BFH-SIGNED-NONVANISHING',['bfh'],14814,14843,[29],'Opening theorem,clauses(i),(ii),pp543–544','The signed quadratic-twist nonvanishing clauses furnish the negative nonzero central value and positive nonzero derivative used here with an arbitrary prescribed finite splitting set.','The actual weight-two modular form, the required functional-equation sign and the stated finite splitting prescriptions; the character specialization is trivial.','First select h_star, then add its primes before selecting D_w. Both choices precede detection. No four-prime bound is assigned to BFH.'),
('HL-FOUR-PRIME-NONVANISHING',['hl'],16538,16579,[56,57],'Theorem,p436,main article','The nonvanishing theorem supplies a fundamental discriminant with nonzero central twist value, at most four prime factors and the stated arbitrary finite splitting set.','Apply to the actual modular elliptic curve after the preliminary signed-prime twist, retaining the finite splitting prescriptions.','The main article alone is used, not Rohrlich appendix. Five primes for the final parameter is the separate CRT/Dirichlet plus one-prime construction. No uniform prime-size bound or requirement that the final product be fundamental.'),
('CST-UNAVERAGED-HEIGHT',['cst'],14844,14891,[30,46],'Theorem1.1,publishedpp2524–2525','The explicit Gross–Zagier height formula relates the unaveraged conductor-one genus point height to the indicated Rankin derivative in the split-level setting.','Actual parametrization and unaveraged point; imaginary quadratic field split at the level, conductor prime to level, no extra units, prescribed genus character.','The factorization uses Lprime(E^D,1)*L(E^h_star,1). Nontorsion at the reference and torsion at nonsimple odd-sign parameters follow with the actual fixed normalization; no class-number averaging.'),
('SILVERMAN-HASSE',['silverman'],14623,14645,[26],'V.Theorem2.3.1,p142','At a good prime, the elliptic Fourier coefficient has absolute value at most2*sqrt(p).','The actual elliptic curve at a good prime.','This is the general bound, not a special CM trace calculation. The strict inequality2*sqrt(p)<p+1 separates the elliptic and unary eigenvalues in the local argument.'),
('SILVERMAN-TORSION-REDUCTION',['silverman'],14855,14891,[30],'VII.Proposition3.1,p192','At good reduction, prime-to-residue-characteristic torsion injects into the residue elliptic curve.','The actual elliptic curve over a number field and a good place; torsion order prime to the residue characteristic.','Apply over every field of degree at most4. Two distinct good rational primes and all residue degrees1through4 produce the displayed fixed annihilator; no special K0 or primes17,23 are imported.'),
]
